{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778946128,"nonce":"nP+lf+QW66LFwe2KQmLYHy+fjC0=","message":"## Design clarification: PTY/tmux control plus transcript/history observation\n\nThe local Claude/Codex CLI path should be modeled as two cooperating planes, not as competing alternatives:\n\n1. Control plane: PTY, tmux, or official CLI attach/resume commands start sessions, send input, resize, interrupt, stop, and reattach.\n2. Observation plane: provider transcript/history files, channel events, structured CLI output, and hooks provide the most veridical semantic events available.\n\nRationale:\n\n- PTY output is what the terminal user sees, but it is not a reliable semantic event stream. It can omit, collapse, redraw, truncate, or visually summarize activity.\n- Claude Code history/transcript files are likely a better source for exact assistant/user/tool turns where available.\n- Channel/MCP events can provide structured Mandible-specific events, but should not be the only way to observe a session.\n- tmux is a pragmatic persistence/control backend. It should not define the ledger or conversation model.\n\nRecommended implementation shape:\n\n```ts\ninterface AgentTransport {\n  start(request): Promise\u003cSessionHandle\u003e;\n  attach(sessionId): Promise\u003cSessionHandle\u003e;\n  sendInput(sessionId, text): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e;\n  sendKey(sessionId, key): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e;\n  interrupt(sessionId): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e;\n  resize(sessionId, cols, rows): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e;\n  stop(sessionId): Promise\u003cvoid\u003e;\n  snapshot(sessionId): Promise\u003cTerminalSnapshot\u003e;\n  events(sessionId): AsyncIterable\u003cAgentRuntimeEvent\u003e;\n}\n\ninterface TranscriptObserver {\n  discover(projectPath): Promise\u003cConversationRef[]\u003e;\n  follow(conversationId): AsyncIterable\u003cConversationEvent\u003e;\n  snapshot(conversationId): Promise\u003cConversationSnapshot\u003e;\n}\n```\n\nTransport backends can include:\n\n- `claude-sdk` for Agent SDK / print-mode structured operation.\n- `claude-pty` for Mandible-owned direct PTY sessions.\n- `claude-tmux` for durable interactive local sessions managed through tmux.\n- `claude-history` as an observer, not a standalone control backend.\n- equivalent Codex/Gemini CLI transports later.\n\nAs of the May 16, 2026 docs, `claude -p` should not be described as simply becoming API-only. More precisely: starting June 15, 2026, Claude Agent SDK usage and `claude -p` non-interactive mode stop drawing from normal subscription usage limits and instead draw from a separate monthly Agent SDK credit for eligible plans, then extra usage if enabled. Interactive Claude Code in terminal/IDE continues to use normal subscription limits. Sources: https://support.claude.com/en/articles/15036540-use-the-claude-agent-sdk-with-your-claude-plan and https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cli-reference.\n\nProduct implication:\n\n- SDK remains useful for structured automation, but should not be the only first-class backend.\n- Local interactive PTY/channel sessions should be first-class and likely default when the user has Claude Code OAuth/subscription but no API key.\n- History/transcript watching should be part of the PTY/tmux implementation, because it supplies the semantic conversation events that terminal output cannot guarantee.\n- The unified Chat/History workspace should bind durable conversations to provider history IDs where possible, while runtime sessions remain resumable control handles.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}